        "pull_request": {
          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/MohamedRejeb/Calf/pulls/339",
          "html_url": "https://github.com/MohamedRejeb/Calf/pull/339",
          "diff_url": "https://github.com/MohamedRejeb/Calf/pull/339.diff",
          "patch_url": "https://github.com/MohamedRejeb/Calf/pull/339.patch",
          "merged_at": "2025-11-01T07:23:28Z"
        },
        "body": "Fixes #205.\r\n\r\nIssues in `BluetoothPermissionHelper.kt`:\r\n- `launchPermissionRequest` was missing the if/else logic (like `getPermissionStatus` does) and was only checking Bluetooth hardware state (`CBManagerStatePoweredOn`) rather than using newer APIs for permission authorization status\r\n- even if `getPermissionStatus` was using the split logic for old/new API, this statement `CBManager.resolveClassMethod(NSSelectorFromString(\"authorization\"))` always returned `false` on my up-to-date iPhone so realistically both methods were using legacy methods for iOS 12 and lower\r\n\r\nFixes:\r\n- add logic for permission authorization status APIs in `launchPermissionRequest`\r\n- decide which API to use (new one is available [since iOS 13](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102267#:~:text=Starting%20in,other%20uses.)) by `systemVersion` property rather than unreliable `resolveClassMethod`\r\n- change invocation for new APIs out of `CBCentralManager`- it doesn't have to be invoked at all (yet, it's still needed to trigger a Permission pop-up)\r\n- add `callbackFired` flag to mitigate multiple callback invocations - basically it should be a one-shot operation if happens.\r\n\r\nI don't do iOS development but I tested these scenarios on my up-to-date real device:\r\n- asking for permissions, then\r\n- granting permissions\r\n- denying permissions\r\n- denying permissions, then changing them in Settings\r\n\r\nAnd everything now works as expected :slightly_smiling_face: ",
